## Every retained owner and backing capacity

The Task1 ledger remains authoritative and unchanged. Define C=sum initialized coefficient slab lengths, R=sum row-buffer lengths, K=descriptor array length, D=outer slab-directory length including spare slots, S=initialized slab count, n=dimension. Owned U payload is P=16C+4R+28K+32n+656, outer payload8n+24D, with n+3S+23 Vector objects and a recorded64-bit struct-field model216B. Add individual Array/GenericMemory headers, backing overreservation/alignment and empty-owner sharing as appropriate. The historical40B/vector reachable-graph model gives G_U=P+8n+24D+40*(n+3S+23)+216 for distinct memory owners. It is not physical allocation size. No Task2 field duplicates coefficients or adds a CSC mirror.

BlockBGFactor has eight fields: base, upper, updates, work, spike, recycled_updates, shared_update_count, row_cache. Seven references and one Int have a64B field model; actual object header/alignment is separate. Its two Float64 vectors work/spike request16n bytes plus two Vector/Memory headers. The updates and recycled_updates vectors each have their own backing capacity, not just length. Each history element contains a reference to a steps vector; charge both container capacity and every distinct steps vector. BartelsGolubStep has two Ints, Bool and Float64; raw scalar sum25B, with ABI padding separately (commonly32B on this64-bit layout, unqueried here). Use actual later sizeof/backing capacities for measured graphs, never25*length as a bound. Shared immutable-by-contract update prefix is counted once in a combined graph, but remains retained by both factors. This private candidate does not retire/recycle a shared prefix; its recycled vector starts empty and has no producer in this scope, so ordinary updates allocate new step vectors as the stopped private reference did.

TriangularRowCache has twelve fields. Owned vectors: order::Vector{Int} (n entries), operations::Vector{Tuple{Int,Int,Float64}} (24B per requested element on the recorded64-bit model), active_upper::Vector{Int} (up to n live entries, possibly larger retained backing), incidence_touched::Vector{Int} (empty/unused by this adapter). Scalar/flag fields are update_count,permuted,upper_dirty,upper_dirty_from,upper_prefix_safe,upper_prefix_candidate; prepared is a reference or nothing. All four Vector/Memory headers, struct fields/padding and spare capacities count. Compiled operations accumulate nonzero history steps; rotations alter order and can cost the sum of rotated intervals during cache synchronization. A refactor or copy starts a fresh cache; it does not copy prepared outputs/compiled operations.

Prepared cache lazily adds one TriangularSpikeCache (two-entry tuple plus next), two PreparedTriangularSpike objects (destination,direction,spike) and four Float64 vectors. Once dimensioned, numeric buffers request32n bytes plus their headers and growth slack. Up to two external destination vectors are retained through provenance references: they are not newly owned copies, but their distinct graph/backing owners must be counted while reachable. Entry identities and exact saved-direction values are required for entering/DSE provenance. No block handle or scratch owner enters this cache. Preparation is attempted even by the private ordinary_forward route, as in the preserved reference; prepare=false prevents spike publication, not the initial cache establishment. Do not describe that route as cache-free.

DirectLowerBackend has nine fields: lower CSC,row_order,scaling,divide_scaling,diagonal,normalize_requested,pivot_policy,fallback,work. Direct mode retains CSC L's colptr(n+1) and rowval/nonzero payload (8(n+1)+16*nnzL under Int64), rows8n, scaling8n, work8n, and optional normalization diagonal8n, plus every CSC/vector/struct/header/backing term. Lower/permutation/scaling/D are immutable by contract and shared on copy; base.work is newly owned. The ordinary factor is not retained in direct mode after setup returns; native release timing is unknown. Fallback mode retains the ordinary backend graph/native ownership, empty lower/rows/scaling/work containers and no D, plus an identity U. A fallback backend copy has its original backend-specific cost; no native-free or graph-size claim is inferred. Both native threshold_one and Markowitz/default use their unchanged requested policies.

Thus a direct factor's requested graph is G_U + manager/work/spike + both history containers/distinct steps + row-cache four containers/fields + optional prepared graph/external destination roots + DirectLowerBackend/L/p/scaling/D/work. Every term is named; physical allocator rounding, backing overreservation of push!/resize!, JIT/compiled code, native backend heap, deferred GC/finalizer roots and OS retention are additional unknown terms. No fixed bytes/event count can be deduced honestly without the later permitted runtime.

## Construction/export/copy/refactor coexistence

Task1 build requests23+n+3S vectors plus BGBlockUpper. Task2 direct constructor adds one lower-work vector, two history vectors, work/spike and four row-cache vectors: nine explicit vector constructors beyond U (plus enclosing/cache/base objects). These counts EXCLUDE backend factorization, exporter extraction, normalization, fallback identity construction, cache first use, boxing and library temporaries. They are source requests, not allocation events. Input B is read by the unchanged backend; there is no retained B field in BlockBGFactor.

Setup validates allowed backend/policy and n+1 Int32 width before factorization; rejects non-Int CSC indexing before backend work. Then one genuine _factorize_basis, one exporter call, optional unchanged _direct_normalize_upper!, one direct bulk U build, and scratch/cache establishment. Genuine export validates L/U triangle/diagonals/finiteness, p/q uniqueness with a seen BitVector, scaling; native additionally uses unchanged _hh_extract_parts/_hh_scale_mode and two work vectors. Those internal/native allocation and solve counts are not newly instrumented or bounded here. Markowitz has its own unchanged extraction. A custom exporter test is not genuine backend-ownership proof.

Normalization first allocates D(n), scans diagonals, allocates candidate values(nnzU), scans/validates all divisions, then copies values into the private exported U on success. Unsafe normalization returns nothing and leaves exported coefficients intact; partially allocated D/values remain transient until collection. Constructor then builds block storage directly from exported U/q, without a packed BG intermediate. During this build ordinary backend + L/U exports/p/q/scaling + normalization temporaries + new blocks/base/cache can coexist. The U export's CSC buffers and q are transient (q values copied into Int32 maps), not retained by block storage. Fallback additionally constructs ones(n), spdiagm identity CSC and collect(1:n) before block import; account for these even though the final U has only n entries.

Copy first copies ALL U retained capacities/holes/scratch through _bgu_copy, then _copy_backend, history container, work/spike and fresh row cache. It shares only read-only lower fields and immutable-by-contract history payload; fallback native backend copying is separate. It creates a fresh empty recycled container and no prepared cache. The source shared_update_count is published only after every explicit allocation succeeds. A failed storage copy leaves all source owner identities and state unchanged. The old graph plus new owned graph and backend-copy temporaries coexist; shared owners are not double-counted in a union graph. Other saved factors are additional roots.

Refactor calls the full constructor privately, with original backend/policy/normalization. It verifies concrete type before publishing all eight fields. Prior factor, new factor, exports, ordinary/native factors, normalization and copied buffers coexist until success/unwind; successful publication resets block high-water/history/cache to the NEW export. No reuse/compaction or mid-update reclamation is introduced. Storage-allocation refusal tests cover each explicit Task1 allocator request, not arbitrary OOM in native routines, Julia boxing, history pushes or cache vectors.

## Per-operation computational work

| Frequency/path | Source work, scans, copies and allocation exposure |
| --- | --- |
| Every FTRAN/BTRAN call | Two constant-time mightalias checks for work/new Float64 edit scratch before dimensions; no scan of every slab owner. Existing supported input/spike alias handling retained. Unsupported scalar/indexed/keyword calls reject before backend. |
| Prepared FTRAN | At most two destination-alias inspections, four resize! requests to establish two slots, row-mapped n spike save, then unchanged fused finite checks/direction copy and publication. Identity/value check when consuming is up to two identity probes and one n isequal scan plus n copy on a hit. First use allocates four numeric buffers/two entries/cache; same dimension may reuse backing but this is unmeasured. |
| FTRAN | One unchanged lower solve/fallback solve. Synchronize only new history (steps plus rotation intervals), then one subtraction per compiled nonzero operation. Map n values into physical work; save n spike; visit active columns descending, each live prefix ascending; one division per active column. Copy/permutation n values to destination. Aliased destination==rhs additionally copies n to work before lower solve. |
| BTRAN | One n input permutation (or n zero fill + unit assignment); active columns ascending, live prefix ascending, unchanged multiply/subtract/divide order. Unit-RHS prefix-safe path divides skipped prefix diagonals and searches active index once. Map n to spike; reverse replay each compiled operation; one unchanged lower/fallback transpose solve. Alias rhs==work or destination==spike adds the preserved n copy. |
| Upper traversal | O(n+visited entries+visited blocks+history), one slab/value/row-array selection per visited block. Last-diagonal test excludes that one entry once; missing/Hessenberg fallback skips matching row per coefficient and uses descending diagonal lookup. Diagonal lookup is O(1) canonical tail, O(column entries+blocks) fallback. No row incidence traversal, coefficient conversion, packing, full-U validation or mirror. Int32 metadata decode does not change Float64 arithmetic. |
| Dirty active columns | One pass from earliest dirty column through n; inspect head/tail/count/last tuple and diagonal/identity predicate. Retained active_upper may resize/push and grow backing. No full column scan for canonical metadata. |
| Replacement preparation | Same tolerance conversion/finite/nonnegative check, dimension, bounds, pivot-magnitude order. Cache hit copies n saved spike. Miss zeros n and reconstructs U*direction with logical columns and prefix entries in original order, O(n+nnzU+blocks), with no arithmetic reassociation. |
| Whole-column removal | Each old block/entry visited once; capture next block before unlink. One row-gap move per incidence, clear one tuple, remove row handle, decrement live, recycle block. No delete-triggered merge or repeated old-column packing. Row-gap distance costs sum over affected rows. |
| Spike insertion | One dense n scan; skip exact zeros only, track nonfinite prefix eligibility, one stable row search per inserted incidence, direct tail predecessor, local insertion/reserve. Row lookup/gap movement can sum row degrees; not O(n) overall. Column-map rotation n-position updates. |
| Pivot/update loop | Original pure-swap predicate/batching, magnitude choice, zero-pivot exception, old/pivot division, exact-zero deletion and history order. _bbg_last_pure scans carried row once only after cheap rejection, plus tested adjacent pivots; row rotation moves interval tuples and maps. |
| Elimination | Monotone source/target incidence ranks in ascending stable cid, original old-m*source with finite tracking, conditional delete/replace/fill. Direct source handle is adjacent-row predecessor, never a generic searching _bgu_set! call. Reload source and target handles after every mutation. Base traversal O(source degree+target degree+fills); add local shifts/splits/merges and gap movement. |
| Structural edit | Nonfull insertion <=31 shifts; relocation adds <=31 copied tuples and old-extent clearing. Split33 staging +33 final tuple writes/backref repairs +31 cleared suffix slots. Delete+one merge <=63 old tuple moves plus clearing. Swaps O(two row degrees); rotation O(affected interval entries+map interval). All Task1 reserve/bin/metadata terms below are additional. |
| Growth under fills | Row reserve copies d handles, zeros only new gap, repairs moved suffix. Fixed capped+8 rule can accumulate Theta(f*d+f^2) copied handles/backref repairs; not hidden in monotone traversal. Seven descriptor arrays grow O(K), three directories O(S), slab append initializes4096B plus descriptors/bins. At most32 bin-head inspections per reserve; no full-arena coefficient copy on append. |
| History publication | At most n-position logical elimination steps before compression; push! may grow step/update containers. Pure rotations compress runs but their map/tuple work still counts. Publish update and prefix eligibility only after success. Failed mutation can leave partial U but no success history; invalidate prepared destinations before mutation and require refactor before later solve. |
| Metadata diagnostics | _block_bg_growth/_finite scan live prefixes only; storage_count scans history lengths. These cold helpers and test full-invariant/materialization scans stay OUTSIDE future timed windows unless symmetrically specified. |

No extra basis solve is introduced by adapter solve/update methods. That means one called lower operation per FTRAN/BTRAN, not a complete native/internal backend-entry budget. Normalization/export and future public controllers remain separate. No new scalar conversion, tolerance, precision, pivot or coefficient-drop policy is introduced. Exception-order changes are limited to explicit private dimension/index/alias gates required by the design, documented and tested before backend work.

